. Unscoped name lookups, repeated every 60 seconds (most likely)

Get-SCOMClassInstance -Name with no class searches DisplayName across every managed entity in the management group.

In Get-WindowsComputerMap:

powershell
$found = @(Get-SCOMClassInstance -Name $slice -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)

In Resolve-WindowsComputer (the per-name fallback):

powershell
$candidates = @(Get-SCOMClassInstance -Name $Fqdn -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) |
              Where-Object { $_.DisplayName -ieq $Fqdn }

Invoke-ApplyPass makes this repeat. Orphan alerts are left open, so the same names are looked up again on every pass for the full 240-minute lookback:

powershell
$criteria = "Name = '$safeName' AND ResolutionState = 0 AND TimeRaised >= '$stamp'"
...
$computer = $computerMap[$fqdn]
if (-not $computer) {
    Write-Log "No Windows Computer instance found for '$fqdn' - skipped." 'WARN'
    $script:Stats.Orphans++
    ...
    continue        # alert stays at ResolutionState 0 -> re-queried next pass
}

Because alerts are sorted oldest-first and capped at MaxBatch, a backlog of orphans fills every batch.

Fix: load the Microsoft.Windows.Computer class instances once per invocation into a hashtable, and cache failed lookups so the same name isn’t searched again for a while.

2. Maintenance-mode churn from restarts

Event 1074 is logged for restarts as well as shutdowns. Every patch reboot therefore gets recursive maintenance mode applied, then released 15 minutes later. Each start and stop writes maintenance-mode history for every hosted object and triggers config recalculation.

In Invoke-MaintenanceMode:

powershell
Start-SCOMMaintenanceMode -Instance $targets -EndTime $EndUtc.ToLocalTime() `
    -Reason PlannedOther -Comment $Comment

In Invoke-SweepPass, the release block:

powershell
$t.Instance.StopMaintenanceMode(
    $stopUtc,
    [Microsoft.EnterpriseManagement.Common.TraversalDepth]::Recursive)

Fix: filter the 1074 rule so it only alerts on power-off, not restart.

3. Possible extend loop (rule out)

If the extend can’t be confirmed, EndUtc is never advanced. The machine then gets the batch extend, a per-machine extend and re-reads on every sweep. Unreadable or misread window end times would cause this, for example a UTC+10 timezone mix-up.

In Invoke-SweepPass:

powershell
$pending = @(Confirm-Extended -Candidates $pending -NewEndUtc $newEnd)
...
$t.Instance.UpdateMaintenanceMode($newEnd, ..., [TraversalDepth]::Recursive)
...
Write-Log "Extend did not take for $($t.Row.Fqdn) - EndUtc left as-is so the next sweep retries." 'ERROR'
4. Smaller per-pass costs
The alert query filters on Name every 60 seconds. Filtering on RuleId would be cheaper.
GetMaintenanceWindow() is called up to three times per tracked machine per sweep, from Get-MaintenanceOwner and Get-MaintenanceEndUtc.
Confirming it from the log

Search ScomShutdownMM.log for each message below:

Log message	Points to
No Windows Computer instance found (high, steady count)	Cause 1
unhosted-object fallback	Cause 1
Applied and Released minutes apart (in the events CSV)	Cause 2
Batch extend confirmed 0 of on every sweep	Cause 3
Extend did not take on every sweep	Cause 3
